response:
 can be solved using the Pythagorean Theorem. Notice that the isosceles trapezoid can be broken down into two right triangles and a rectangle. [asy]
unitsize(1.5mm);
defaultpen(linewidth(.7pt)+fontsize(10pt));
dotfactor=3;

pair A=(0,0), B=(21,0), C=(15,8), D=(6,8);
pair[] dots={A,B,C,D};

draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
dot(dots);
label("A",A,SW);
label("B",B,SE);
label("C",C,NE);
label("D",D,NW);
label("9",midpoint(C--D),N);
label("10",midpoint(D--A),NW);
label("21",midpoint(A--B),S);
label("10",midpoint(B--C),NE);
label("7",midpoint(A--B),NW);
label("8",midpoint(C--D),NE);

draw(rightanglemark(A,C,D,18));
draw(rightanglemark(B,C,D,18));
[/asy]
Now, label the figure as seen in the diagram. We see that $AC$ is the hypotenuse of right triangle $ABC$ and is equal to $10$. Let $AB=x$ and $CD=y$. Since $AD=BC$, we have $$2x+(y-x)=21.$$ A line of symmetry would map $AB\mapsto CD$ with $A\mapsto D$ and $B\mapsto C$, so $21-x=21-y$ and therefore $$2x=y.$$ Thus, $2x=21-(2x)$ and $x=7$. So, $y=21-7=14$, then $x+14=21$. But $y=CD$, and therefore $CD=14$.

