Among the 81 squares, picking one in each row and column means that we'll pick all the numbers from a valid permutation of the numbers 1 through 8.  So, all we need to do is minimize

\[
\sum_{k=1}^{8} \frac{1}{8 + 1 - k} = \sum_{k=1}^{8} \frac{1}{9 - k}
\]

without any permutations, i.e., adding up the reciprocal of the units digits of the losing positions in the game of NIM on the NIM heap of 8 counters.  The game is impartial, so we can use the mex function of the NIM theory: mex \{$S_1, S_2, \cdots, S_k \}$ = smallest integer not in the set $\{S_1, S_2, \cdots, S_k\}$.  We can use the mex to efficiently obtain the answer for a given input.  The mex function's return value indicates the number of stones that will be eventually removing from the heap, ifplyed in NIM strategy.
